tes  index it's actually a number that is  sort of how good is this arm how good is  this job to serve what if you don't  exactly compute that number because it  could be hard to compute the optimality  proofs don't uh give us a good idea and  then the last thing is that all of these  settings are cases that I'll call like  they have a fully specified stochastic  model one way to say say this is that  you can formalize this as an mdp where  you know all of the parameters typically  just solving that mdp is kind of too  expensive which is why we care about  things like the Gibson index which gives  us a sort of neater solutions to  optimality but what if we don't have  full knowledge of all the parameters  okay so and the way I sort of view all  of these questions is they all are  instances of or at least kind of related  to versions of this question which is do  near Jason's policies have near Optimal  Performance so as an example I think of  multiple multi-server uh scheduling or  multi-server multi-unbounded as well if  I'm pulling the two best arms uh that's  kind of like pulling the best arm you  know twice as hard roughly speaking so  that's kind of nearly up to nearly  getting Z so is that nearly optimal and  uh in my thesis I studied this question  in the context of scheduling in queues  and so that's what I'm going to focus on  today so let me tell you a bit about  what scheduling in the queues even looks  like so this is a single server queue uh  we have a server that can serve jobs  that are arriving over time jobs that  are waiting for service will be in this  queue and this is a job so a job is  going to be a test tube its height is  its size that's how long it needs to be  served for and we'll represent that  service as filling the test tube with  water and so we can talk about the  progress we've made so far in a job it's  age and then the remaining work called  its remaining size  and we have these jobs arriving over  time According to some stochastic