  way of thinking about queuing that's  called The Mark of process jump model  and these are and the both of these  ideas are things I think uh might have  analogs outside of queuing  um so I'd be happy to discuss them and  then the last theme that I think is  really important is uh thinking about  this fully specified area and there's  lots of open questions here so one  example would be I said it's reasonable  to assume we know the job size  distribution what if we've learned the  job size distribution from data we don't  know the exact distribution  how can we adapt cases for that setting  do we need a distributionally robust  version of gits what does that even look  like  um I Know It uh I I have started  thinking about this problem but it's  very hard and I'm very interested to  collaborate with folks on these sorts of  things anyway thanks for your attention  happy to take questions  [Applause]  thanks for the lovely talk questions was  Eve and maybe the next speaker can set  up meanwhile as well  how do we know that it's nearer  uh can you repeat the question yeah sure  so the question is uh what do we even  know about the optimal solution in the  multi-server setting the answer is we  don't know very much what we actually  show is that uh gations with multiple  servers in certain uh under some  generous some generous assumptions it  actually approaches something even  better than the optimal multi-server  policy it approaches single server cases  if you smush all the servers together  into one big bad server which is even  better than having a multi-server system  um  and actually exact lower bounds on the  multi-server optimal are still an open  question in QA  maybe time for another question does any  no thanks again and go to the next talk  [Music]  yeah so the next stop is an incentive  aware machine learning for decision  making by Chada

